Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The orchestration occurs at two points in the request processing flow:\\n\\n1. Session loading (open_session): In `RequestContext.push()` in `src/flask/ctx.py` (lines 380-389). Called when the request context is pushed at the start of handling, after the application context is established and before URL matching. The code shows:\\n   ```python\\n   if self.session is None:\\n       session_interface = self.app.session_interface\\n       self.session = session_interface.open_session(self.app, self.request)\\n   ```\\n   This happens in `Flask.wsgi_app()` (line 1510 in `src/flask/app.py`) when `ctx.push()` is called.\\n\\n2. Session saving (save_session): In `Flask.process_response()` in `src/flask/app.py` (lines 1321-1322). Called after response generation, after `after_request` functions, and before the request context is popped. The code shows:\\n   ```python\\n   if not self.session_interface.is_null_session(ctx.session):\\n       self.session_interface.save_session(self, ctx.session, response)\\n   ```\\n   This is invoked from `Flask.finalize_request()` (line 941), which is called after `full_dispatch_request()` completes.\\n\\nThe lifecycle documentation (`docs/lifecycle.rst`, lines 127-128 and 147-148) confirms:\\n- Step 6: \\"The session is opened, loading any existing session data using the app\'s session_interface\\"\\n- Step 15: \\"The session is saved, persisting any modified session data using the app\'s session_interface\\"\\n\\nRegarding concurrent requests and race conditions, `SessionInterface` documentation (`src/flask/sessions.py`, lines 141-146) states: \\"Multiple requests with the same session may be sent and handled concurrently. When implementing a new session interface, consider whether reads or writes to the backing store must be synchronized. There is no guarantee on the order in which the session for each request is opened or saved, it will occur in the order that requests begin and end processing.\\"\\n\\nThe orchestration relies on per-request context isolation (via `contextvars`) so each request has its own session instance. Synchronization for concurrent access to shared session storage is the responsibility of the `SessionInterface` implementation."}' + '\n')
